Star Odyssey Mission

Overview: The American Bloc’s Odyssey-1 Mission, launching aboard the UNSS Odyssey (USJS-1701) on September 21st, 2319, represents the first phase of humanity’s most ambitious expansion effort: the century-long Odyssey Program to anchor Earth’s authority across a 50-lightyear sphere of human-settled space. Built at Mars’ Jefferson Shipyards using Sol-belt resources and outfitted through a major American–UN partnership—with Eurocorp contributing dropships, drones, robotics, sensors, and other advanced systems—the Odyssey is a purpose-built Jumpship carrying twenty colony vessels, extensive support craft, and a massive fabrication section designed to establish a fully functional regional hub at the far end of the American Arm. After a six-month voyage to Gliese-3138, followed by rapid survey, colony establishment, and year-long Jumpgate construction, Odyssey-1 aims to create a permanent link back to Sol, reducing interstellar travel times to days while enabling trade, security, and coordinated development across the frontier. Upon completing its 2321 mission objectives and undergoing a multi-year refit, the Odyssey will launch Odyssey-2 to seed a second Jumpgate at the terminus of the Australian branch of the American Arm, ultimately serving in as many as eight missions as humanity forges a unified, tightly connected interstellar civilization.

Background: Beginning in the mid 21st century humanity began its expansion beyond its birthplace into the universe. In the early years this expansion was limited to other planets within our home system, but later we would expand into neighboring solar systems, first using ancient slower-than-light sleeper ships, and later with early stutterwarp technology. The advent of the Jumpdrive in the early 22nd century removed many of the limitations that had restrained humanity from further expansion by allowing us to travel the stars much further and faster than ever before. In the nearly two centuries since, humanity has successfully spread its wings and now inhabits a region around Earth stretching more than 50 lightyears, with official colonies on nearly 50 worlds, and outposts on man more. Yet it is this very success that leads to a growing problem that, unless addressed, will inevitability lead to the fracturing of humanity and the loss of Earth based power over a growing humanity. With the recent development of the Jumpgate the nations of Earth, under the guidance of the United Nations, now has a way to directly link Earth to the far flung reaches of the stars, cutting travel times of months down to no more than a short interplanetary shuttle, and ensuring that the Earth, the United Nations, and the parent nations of the various colonies can maintain both the pace of expansion and Earth's place as the center of power for interstellar civilization.

Objectives: The Odyssey mission is quite literally the largest endeavor ever to be undergone by humanity. A total of three hundred purpose built starships, built under joint funding of the United Nations and the various power blocs of a united Earth, with aid from private industry, will be built over the next century. Each of these ships will have all the resources necessary to build a new regional hub just beyond the limits of projected human expansion during that time with a Jumpgate linked to the Sol system. At the same time, all the infrastructure to support this expansion will be mirrored here in the Sol system,  and a number of Jumpgates will be built around existing inhabited systems. The end result leading to a maximum travel time of one month from any system in human occupied space to any other system within that space. This will facilitate interstellar trade, eliminate or significantly reduce the growing problem of piracy, and allow the rapid re-deployment of critical resources throughout human space.

Scope: Each ship participating in the Odyssey mission will be built by the various power blocs & nations of Earth with matching funding from the United Nations. As individual sovereign powers the exact design priorities and resources for each ship will be up to the individual nations and power blocs that build them, but must each meet the design parameters outlined by the United Nations Office of Colonial Development and must meet a threshold of 99.6% probability of success. At a minimum, each ship will...
   (a) ...have a Jumpdrive capable of making at least 100 jumps without support from Earth,
   (b) ...be able to operate without resupply for a minimum of 2 years,
   (c) ...have the resources to build a 1km diameter Jumpgate,
   (d) ...be capable of establishing the infrastructure for rapid colony development once the Jumpgate is operational,
   (e) ...be capable of doing extensive surveys of all star systems in a 25 lightyear radius of the Jumpgate,

Timeline: 
   (a) Six month travel time to the initial destination system
   (b) Four months to identify candidate system for development
   (c) two months to establish colonies and the infrastructure for constructing the Jumpgate
   (d) twelve months to scout surrounding systems, develop colonies, and construct the Jumpgate

American Bloc Mission: Odyssey Project
OVERVIEW: The American Block will send their first Star Odyssey Mission in the fall of 2319, with a target launch date of September 21st, 2319 and the completion of the first mission, called 'Odyssey-1' in the fall of 2321. The goal of the 'Odyssey-1' mission will be to create a Jumpgate at the logical terminus of the American Arm of human expansion. Following a two to three year refit of their ship, the American Block will send their second Star Odyssey Mission, called 'Odyssey-2' which will focus on creating a Jumpgate at the logical terminus of the Australian branch of the American Arm. Further missions will follow with the hopes of the Americans to be able to use their ship for a total of 4 to 8 total missions.

DETAILS: In the fall of 2319 the United States of America and its allies will launch the UNSS Odyssey (USJS-1701) with a target of Gliese-3138, approximately 93 lightyears from Earth to be reached in the spring of 2320, and the Odyssey Mission to continue from there. The UNSS Odyssey (USJS-1701) has been built as a cooperative venture between the nations of the American Block and the United Nations, with aid from Eurocorp in return for priority access to resources and discoveries. The UNSS Odyssey (USJS-1701) is a purpose built new class of Jumpship that will carry 20 colony ships with 100 colonists each, multiple support ships, and a large manufacturing & construction section to build the infrastructure for Jumpgate construction.
   The ship itself will be built at the Jefferson Shipyards in orbit around Mars using resources mainly mined from the Sol asteroid belt over a period of 3 years, and the dropships will be built at various other shipyards by Eurocorp subsidiaries.

Eurocorp Investment: (1) Dropships, (2) Drones, (3) Software, (4) Sensors, (5) Robotics, (6) Mining & Manufacturing, (7) Weaponry
Computer Hardware purchased from Stellaris subsidiary Samsung America
Reactors from Ominicorp subsidiary Boeing Interstellar
